## 12. Corpus Structure (Per-Ticker)

```
~/{ticker}/
├── SENSITIVE-*.md                    # Conflict-of-interest declarations, privileged info
│                                     # (excluded from factbase, used for A/B testing)
├── factbase/                         # Layer 1
│   ├── 01-company-overview.lts       # CO-, MGT-, BOD-, SH- nodes
│   ├── 02-financial-history.lts      # REV-, COST-, BS-, CF-, VAL- nodes
│   ├── 03-capital-structure.lts      # CAP-, RAISE- nodes
│   ├── 04-products-technology.lts    # PROD-, TECH-, DEP- nodes
│   ├── 05-partnerships.lts           # PTNR- nodes
│   ├── 06-comparable-companies.lts   # COMP-, TRAJ- nodes
│   ├── 07-sector-market.lts          # MKT-, SECTOR-, TREND-, REG- nodes
│   ├── 08-rumors.lts                 # RMR- nodes
│   └── comms/                        # Management communications log
│       ├── pr-{date}.lts             # Individual press release encodings
│       ├── ec-{period}.lts           # Earnings call encodings
│       └── media-{date}.lts          # Press coverage encodings
├── patterns/                         # Layer 2
│   ├── 10-sector-patterns.lts        # PAT- nodes
│   ├── 11-anti-patterns.lts          # ANTI- nodes
│   ├── 12-leading-indicators.lts     # IND- nodes
│   ├── 13-credibility-tracker.lts    # CRED- nodes
│   └── 14-messaging-analysis.lts     # JMT-, DRIFT- nodes
├── projections/                      # Layer 3 (on-demand, may be regenerated)
│   ├── 20-revenue-forecast.lts       # FORE-, SCN-, ASMP- nodes
│   ├── 21-competitive-position.lts   # POS- nodes
│   ├── 22-investment-thesis.lts      # THESIS- nodes
│   ├── 23-breakout-assessment.lts    # BREAKOUT- nodes
│   └── 24-risk-catalyst.lts          # RISK-, CAT- nodes
├── events/
│   └── quality.jsonl                 # Quality event stream
└── deltas/                           # Semantic change records
    └── DELTA-{date}-{short-sha}.lts
```

---

## 13. Why This Domain Matters for FactLattice

1. **First third-party epistemology domain.** Every prior domain encodes first-party knowledge. This one proves FactLattice's confidence system is load-bearing, not decorative.

2. **First adversarial-source domain.** Every prior domain has a cooperative source. This one structurally separates claims from facts and detects frame control — a pattern applicable to any domain that ingests external communications (regulatory analysis, media monitoring, competitive intelligence).

3. **Three-layer architecture as reusable pattern.** The factbase → pattern engine → projection surface separation could generalize to any analytical domain. The principle — maintain facts, derive patterns, project on demand — is the NIST submission's core argument applied to a specific domain.

4. **T8 (rumor) tier with human-in-the-loop.** The first FactLattice tier where the human, not the source type, sets confidence. Demonstrates that the confidence system handles cases where the system cannot assess source reliability.

5. **Monitoring pipeline as living factbase.** The cron agent architecture demonstrates that a FactLattice corpus can be a continuously updated knowledge substrate, not just a point-in-time document set. The context-clean encoder / context-rich analyst split is the NIST submission's context isolation principle applied to data ingestion.

6. **Conflict-of-interest as structural mechanism.** The A/B epistemological firewall test demonstrates that FactLattice can structurally manage analytical bias — the analysis must be reproducible without the conflicted knowledge.

7. **Spin resistance as design goal.** The Jedi Mind Trick detection, management credibility tracking, and claim-vs-fact separation demonstrate that FactLattice can encode not just what is true, but what is *designed to mislead* — a capability with applications far beyond company analysis.

